# Line endings are the repository's decision, not each machine's.
#
# Without this file, what gets committed depends on the core.autocrlf setting
# of whichever computer happens to be committing. That is fine while there is
# one computer. With two it is a standing invitation to a diff in which every
# line of a file has changed and none of its content has, which is exactly what
# it looked like here after one machine's editor saved a file with Windows
# endings and the other's did not.
#
# text=auto: git decides what is text, normalises it to LF inside the
# repository, and leaves the working tree to the platform. Nothing on disk has
# to change and no editor has to be reconfigured.
* text=auto

# Explicitly text, so these are never guessed at.
*.js    text
*.jsx   text
*.json  text
*.css   text
*.html  text
*.md    text
*.svg   text

# Explicitly binary, so git never tries to normalise them and never offers a
# line-by-line diff of an image.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
